# Simfinity Maintenance

## Documentation

- All documentation lives in a single `README.md` at the project root
- Do NOT create separate README files for individual features
- When making structural changes, update the README's project structure section
- When adding new APIs or patterns, add examples to the relevant README section

## Skills

Available Cursor skills for this project are in `~/.cursor/skills/`:
- `simfinity-next-scaffold` — scaffold a new project from this template
- `simfinity-add-entity` — add a new entity with all customization files
- `simfinity-custom-component` — create a custom component with story + barrel export
- `simfinity-form-customization` — add form customization for an entity mode
- `simfinity-column-renderer` — register a custom column renderer
- `simfinity-state-machine` — set up an entity state machine
- `simfinity-i18n` — add i18n labels for an entity

When making structural changes to the project (folder names, registration APIs, file patterns), update the relevant skills to match.

## Storybook

- Every custom component in `src/components/custom/` must have a story
- Stories use `@storybook/nextjs-vite` with MUI theme decorators (`.storybook/preview.tsx`)
- Form input stories: Default, WithContent, WithError, Disabled
- Column display stories: Default, NullValue, edge cases
- Run `npm run storybook` to verify after adding/modifying components

## Cursor Rules

- Rules in `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` should reflect the current project structure
- When renaming folders, changing APIs, or updating dependencies, update the relevant rule files
- Keep the naming conventions tables up to date
